Clear passwords saved while there was nowhere safe to keep them.

OSCrypt on this platform has only PosixKeyProvider, whose key is a constant
compiled into every Chromium build, so Login Data is obfuscated rather than
encrypted and anyone with the file can read it offline.
ohos-password-manager-off.patch stopped new passwords being written, but
whatever a user saved before that is still on disk and still readable, and the
manager being off means they cannot even be used -- they are liability and
nothing else.

Swept once per profile at startup, remembered in a pref so a restart does not
repeat it. The sweep is conditioned on PasswordsAreEncrypted(), which returns
false today and becomes true with the HUKS key provider: after that there is
nothing left that the old key wrote, and migration rather than deletion is the
right answer.

Chosen over a shell command because it does not depend on the shell
remembering to call it, or on the user opening settings, and because whether
the passwords are protected is something only this side knows.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

